---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const mlpicCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Supervised: learning from labels', note: 'Examples with answers (spam / not-spam, price / sold price) teach a mapping from inputs to outputs. The dominant mode of applied ML — and of the classification and regression lesson next door.' },
    { math: '2 · Unsupervised: finding structure', note: 'No labels — just data. Clustering (k-means), dimensionality reduction (PCA), anomaly detection. The algorithm decides what "similar" means; you decide whether the structure it found means anything.' },
    { math: '3 · Reinforcement: learning from reward', note: 'An agent acts, the environment scores it, and behaviour evolves toward higher reward. Games (AlphaGo), robotics, recommendation tuning. Powerful, sample-hungry, hard to specify safely.' },
    { math: '4 · Self-supervised: the modern bridge', note: 'Predict masked words or neighbouring image patches — labels generated from the data itself. This is how LLMs and vision models pretrain on the internet without hand-labelling, then fine-tune for tasks.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/intermediate/machine-learning-in-one-picture">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The AI field organises itself into four learning paradigms, defined by <strong>where the
    learning signal comes from</strong>: labelled examples (supervised), raw structure
    (unsupervised), reward (reinforcement), or the data itself (self-supervised). Everything
    from spam filters to ChatGPT fits one of these boxes — and knowing which box tells you what
    data you need and what can go wrong.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four paradigms, defined by their learning signal" widget="equationStep" config={mlpicCfg} />

  <h2>Choosing a paradigm by problem shape</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Have labelled history?</strong> Supervised: churn prediction, fraud, pricing. The workhorse — most business ML is this.</li>
    <li><strong>No labels, want structure?</strong> Unsupervised: customer segments, document grouping, anomalies. Results need human interpretation — clusters are suggestions, not truths.</li>
    <li><strong>Sequential decisions with feedback?</strong> Reinforcement: control, game-playing, page optimisation. Great power, fragile training (the RL lesson covers it).</li>
    <li><strong>Massive raw data, few labels?</strong> Self-supervised pretrain + fine-tune — the recipe behind every modern foundation model.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, Deep Learning; OpenAI, "Learning from human preferences"; LeCun, "A Path Towards Autonomous Machine Intelligence".</p>
</TopicPage>
